This is a major update where we have comprehensively overhauled the way we handle raw byte storage in the codebase. We have shifted from using std::string and std::string_view to std::vector<uint8_t>, std::array<uint8_t> and std::span<uint8_t>. This change is motivated by a desire to enhance clarity, efficiency, and robustness in handling raw bytes, especially in the context of TCP sockets which operate on uint8_t arrays.

Our new byte storage and viewing approach employs the following:

```cpp
using Byte = uint8_t;
using Bytes = std::vector<Byte>;
template <std::size_t N>
using BytesArr = std::array<Byte, N>;
using BytesArrView = std::span<const Byte, std::dynamic_extent>;
using BytesArrMutableView = std::span<Byte, std::dynamic_extent>;
```

The breadth of the modification impacts almost every aspect of our code. Alongside the primary change, we've also introduced several enhancements and adjustments to provide better context, such as duplicating some ABI functions to accommodate both Bytes and std::string, refactoring FixedString to FixedArr and opting for std::array instead (take note that std::array cannot leverage std::move as uint8_t is not movable), and incorporating new utility functions. Numerous functions now also take std::span as an argument. I would kindly ask reviewers to pay special attention to the utils.h and strings.h classes.

The most critical alteration concerns ethCallInfo, which now includes an additional Functor variable in its tuple. Moreover, the data attribute of ethCallInfo now returns a BytesArrView, necessitating careful consideration of data ownership. The ethCallInfo structure is now as follows:

```
/// ethCallInfo: tuple of (from, to, gasLimit, gasPrice, value, functor, data)
/// **ATTENTION**: Be aware that we are using BytesArrView, so you MUST be sure that the data allocated in the BytesArrView is valid during the whole life of the tuple.
/// If you need ethCallInfo to own the data, use ethCallInfoAllocated instead.
using ethCallInfo = std::tuple<Address,Address,uint256_t, uint256_t, uint256_t, Functor, BytesArrView>;
/// This is the same as ethCallInfo, but using Bytes instead of BytesArrView, truly allocating the data, some places need it such as tests.
using ethCallInfoAllocated = std::tuple<Address,Address,uint256_t, uint256_t, uint256_t, Functor, Bytes>;
```

When using std::span, it's essential to remember that it does not own the data. This means that you need to ensure the integrity of the array that std::span is referencing for the span's duration, ethCallInfoAllocated is used when needed (such as tests). To append the return of a function to bytes, it's recommended to use Utils::appendBytes.

Given the extent of the changes, we acknowledge the possibility of conflicts, and I take full responsibility for their resolution. Your detailed review and feedback on this extensive refactor will be highly appreciated.

